{{also|blackout|Blackout|black-out|Black-out}}
==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en|en-au-black out.ogg|Audio (AU)}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|*}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[censor]] or [[cover up]] by writing over with black ink.
#* '''2006''', Calton Lewis, Wake Island: The Story of a Civilian Pow at Niigata, Japan 1941-1945
#*: Seigel was able to send two post cards home in 44 months via the Red Cross. The first one was completely '''blacked out''' by the Japanese censor's pen. The second one he sent, Seigel wrote that he weighed his "usual weight of 130". Seigel's usual weight was 230 so the family could see that he had been severely starved while in captivity. Seigel had at least triumphed over the Japanese censor and his black pen.
# {{lb|en|transitive|figuratively}} To [[censor]] or [[cover up]].
#* {{quote-song|en|year=1979|author=w:Chris de Burgh|title=The Devil's Eye|album={{w|Crusader (Chris de Burgh album)|Crusader}}|passage=I have '''blacked out''' your television, every station in the world is mine.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Simon Parkin|title=README.txt by Chelsea Manning review – secrets and spies|work=The Guardian|date=2022-10-27|url=https://www.theguardian.com/books/2022/oct/27/readmetxt-by-chelsea-manning-review-secrets-and-spies|issn=0261-3077|passage=At times, README.txt is vague; some sections have been '''blacked out''', presumably on legal advice.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[lose]] [[consciousness]]; to suffer a [[blackout]].
#: {{ant|en|black in#Verb|come to#Verb}}
#*{{quote-book|en|author=Charles A. Lindbergh|authorlink=Charles Lindbergh|date=3 June 1944|title=The Wartime Journals of Charles A. Lindbergh|location=New York|publisher=w:Harcourt Brace Jovanovich|year_published=1970|page=839|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/wartimejournalso0000lind/page/839|text=I am '''blacking out'''—ease the stick forward slightly—the sky is clear again—I turn out over water—reverse turn—there are antiaircraft on the island below—I reverse again—and again.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1956|author=w:Andre Norton|title=w:Plague Ship
|passage=And they were not surprised when Tang Ya reeled into the mess, his face livid and drawn with pain. Rip and Dane got him to his cabin before he '''blacked out'''. But all they could learn from him during the interval before he lost consciousness was that his head was bursting and he couldn't stand it. Over his limp body they stared at one another bleakly.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[obscure]] in darkness.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1924|author=w:Richard Connell|title=w:The Most Dangerous Game
|passage=Bleak darkness was '''blacking out''' the sea and jungle when Rainsford sighted the lights. He came upon them as he turned a crook in the coast line; and his first thought was that be had come upon a village, for there were many lights.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1963|author=w:Margery Allingham|title=w:The China Governess
|chapter=14|url=http://openlibrary.org/works/OL2004261W
|passage=Nanny Broome was looking up at the outer wall.&nbsp; Just under the ceiling there were three lunette windows, heavily barred and '''blacked out''' in the normal way by centuries of grime.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=May 10 1943|journal=w:Pittsburgh Press|title=Press Serial Story: Girl In A Blackout
|passage=The Venetian blinds and the drapes, she thought, would completely '''black out''' the room from the ocean side.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To be in a state of blackout, as a building, a city, a ship.

